Adapt investigation focus based on codebase type:
- **Modeling codebase** (training code, model definitions): training data formats, evaluation requirements and metrics, label generation pipelines, data augmentation strategies, data quality requirements and measurement, negative sampling, feature crosses and interaction signals, embedding tables, dataset versioning, and experiment-to-data lineage.
- **Serving / business codebase** (inference endpoints, application logic): map the end-to-end dataflow of how production data interacts with models — from request ingestion through feature assembly, inference, response, and post-serving logging. Investigate applicable live-traffic feedback signals (e.g., clicks, conversions, dwell time, explicit ratings — not all are applicable to every use case) and how they feed back into training data (consider user data policies and whether user data requires anonymization or simulation). Identify opportunities for data-driven model improvement: what deep data analysis could be performed (distribution drift, segment-level performance, error mining, counterfactual analysis), what new data sources could be tapped, and how the feedback loop from serving to training could be tightened. Also cover feature serving latency, online/offline feature consistency, A/B test traffic splits, and real-time data freshness.